From cef8430c47ef29c4903511569a92197c242b3ba5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Majid Arasskhani <M.Arasskhani@gosilkroad.net>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 13:51:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 03/10] fix(deploy): never rotate POSTGRES_PASSWORD in .env
 without changing it in the cluster

apply_prod_mode ran ensure_secret on POSTGRES_PASSWORD like every other
secret. Unlike the others, that password also lives inside the alpha_router_pg
volume as the role password. On a host installed with --dev (.env.example ships
'changeme') and later upgraded with --prod, .env got a fresh password while the
cluster kept the old one: pgbouncer and db-init failed authentication and
wait_for_health died after 900s with no automatic way back.

rotate_postgres_password() now:
- leaves any operator-chosen value alone (unchanged behaviour);
- rotates .env freely only when no alpha_router_pg volume exists (initdb will
  create the role with the new value);
- otherwise starts only the postgres service if needed, runs
  ALTER ROLE ... WITH PASSWORD over the unix socket, and writes .env only
  after that succeeds; on failure it dies and leaves .env untouched so the
  stack still starts.

scripts/tests/test-deploy-mode-pg-rotation.sh covers the five paths with
mocked docker/compose; new CI job 'scripts-tests' runs scripts/tests/*.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UTnpHajSmkk2JMLfuQoV7o
